window.AG_PRACTICE["liuzijue"] = {"id":"liuzijue","axis":"daoyin","seal":"導","scene":"art/scene-garden.jpg","title":"Six Healing Sounds","titleZh":"六字訣","sub":"Six breaths, six organs — the oldest breathing practice in the canon","subZh":"六息六臟——吐納之最古者","mentor":{"key":"xiong","nm":"Xióng Shī","nmZh":"熊師","line":"\"You do not need to be loud. The sound shapes the breath, and the breath does the work. Whisper it if you like — the shape of your mouth is the whole technique.\"","lineZh":"「不必出大聲。字是用來塑那口氣的，做事的是氣。想輕聲就輕聲——口型本身就是全部的功夫。」"},"card":{"h":"Six Sounds, Six Organs 六字六臟","hZh":"","p":"XU for the Liver, HE for the Heart, HU for the Spleen, SI for the Lung, CHUI for the Kidney, XI for the San Jiao. Each sound shapes the exhale differently, and each is paired with one organ.","pZh":"噓屬肝、呵屬心、呼屬脾、呬屬肺、吹屬腎、嘻屬三焦。六字口型各異，故其出息亦異，各配一臟。"},"note":"Educational content. Breathe gently and never force or hold the breath; stop and rest if you feel light-headed. 本組為教學內容。呼吸宜緩，勿強勿閉氣；若覺頭暈，即止而歇。","items":[{"k":"match","q":"Match each sound to its organ.","qZh":"六字配五臟三焦。","pairs":[{"l":"XŪ 噓","lZh":"","r":"Liver","rZh":"肝"},{"l":"HĒ 呵","lZh":"","r":"Heart","rZh":"心"},{"l":"HŪ 呼","lZh":"","r":"Spleen","rZh":"脾"},{"l":"SÌ 呬","lZh":"","r":"Lung","rZh":"肺"},{"l":"CHUĪ 吹","lZh":"","r":"Kidney","rZh":"腎"}],"note":"The sixth is XĪ 嘻 for the San Jiao, which closes the set by settling qi through all three burners. 其六為嘻，屬三焦，以之收束，令三焦之氣俱和。"},{"k":"order","q":"Put the six sounds in their classical order.","qZh":"排出六字之常序。","seq":[{"t":"XŪ 噓 · Liver","c":"噓 · 肝"},{"t":"HĒ 呵 · Heart","c":"呵 · 心"},{"t":"HŪ 呼 · Spleen","c":"呼 · 脾"},{"t":"SÌ 呬 · Lung","c":"呬 · 肺"},{"t":"CHUĪ 吹 · Kidney","c":"吹 · 腎"},{"t":"XĪ 嘻 · San Jiao","c":"嘻 · 三焦"}],"note":"Liver, Heart, Spleen, Lung, Kidney, San Jiao — the five zang in generating-cycle order (Wood, Fire, Earth, Metal, Water), then the San Jiao to close. Recognising that the order IS the generating cycle makes it impossible to forget. 肝心脾肺腎，正相生之序（木火土金水），末以三焦收之。知其序即相生之序，則終身不忘。"},{"k":"mc","q":"During each sound, when do you make it?","qZh":"六字之發，在何時？","opts":[{"t":"On the exhale only; the inhale is silent and through the nose","c":"惟於呼氣時發之，吸則靜而由鼻","ok":1,"note":"Inhale quietly through the nose, exhale with the mouth shaped for the sound. The sound can be silent — the mouth shape alone changes how the air leaves, which is the mechanism. Never force the exhale to its very end. 鼻吸宜靜，口呼而作其形。其聲可無，惟口型一變，其出息即異，此即其法。呼不宜盡。"},{"t":"On the inhale","c":"吸氣時","ok":0,"note":"The sounds are exhalation techniques throughout. 六字皆為呼法。"},{"t":"While holding the breath","c":"閉氣時","ok":0,"note":"Breath-holding is not part of this practice and is not advisable. 此法不閉氣，亦不宜閉氣。"},{"t":"Continuously through both","c":"呼吸皆發","ok":0,"note":"Only the exhale carries the sound. 惟出息載其聲。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"Which sound would traditionally be chosen for someone who is irritable with tight ribs and dry eyes?","qZh":"其人煩躁、脅緊、目乾，傳統當取何字？","opts":[{"t":"XŪ 噓 — the Liver sound","c":"噓 — 肝之字","ok":1,"note":"Irritability, rib-side tightness and dry eyes all belong to the Liver, and XU is its sound. Traditionally it is paired with looking gently forward or slightly widening the eyes on the exhale, since the Liver opens into the eyes. Treat this as a calming practice, not a treatment. 煩躁、脅緊、目乾，俱屬肝，故取噓。傳統於出息時目微張前視，以肝開竅於目故。此為靜心之法，非治病之術。"},{"t":"CHUĪ 吹","c":"吹","ok":0,"note":"CHUI is the Kidney sound — for cold, low back and fear-related presentations. 吹屬腎，主寒、腰、恐。"},{"t":"SÌ 呬","c":"呬","ok":0,"note":"SI is the Lung sound — for grief, a tight chest and the breath. 呬屬肺，主悲、胸悶、呼吸。"},{"t":"HŪ 呼","c":"呼","ok":0,"note":"HU is the Spleen sound — for overthinking, poor appetite and a heavy middle. 呼屬脾，主思慮、納呆、中焦困重。"}]},{"k":"match","q":"Match each sound to the emotion its organ governs.","qZh":"六字配其臟所主之志。","pairs":[{"l":"XŪ 噓 (Liver)","lZh":"","r":"Anger, frustration","rZh":"怒"},{"l":"HĒ 呵 (Heart)","lZh":"","r":"Agitation, restless joy","rZh":"喜／煩"},{"l":"HŪ 呼 (Spleen)","lZh":"","r":"Overthinking, worry","rZh":"思"},{"l":"SÌ 呬 (Lung)","lZh":"","r":"Grief, sadness","rZh":"悲憂"},{"l":"CHUĪ 吹 (Kidney)","lZh":"","r":"Fear","rZh":"恐"}],"note":"This is why the practice is often taught as an emotional regulation tool rather than a physical one. It is supportive, gentle and free — and it is not a substitute for care when someone is genuinely struggling. 故此法多以調情志而傳，不以治形體而傳。其性和緩、無費、可自為；然人若真有所苦，此不能代其所當得之照顧。"},{"k":"mc","q":"How long is the practice usually held for each sound?","qZh":"每字行之，其數幾何？","opts":[{"t":"Six repetitions each, adjusted to what feels comfortable","c":"每字六息，隨其安適而增減","ok":1,"note":"Six of each is the common teaching, with more repetitions of the sound matching the current complaint. Comfort governs — if you feel light-headed, you are pushing the exhale too far or breathing too deeply. 常法每字六息，若某臟有所苦，則增其字之數。以安適為度：若覺頭暈，是呼之太盡或息之太深。"},{"t":"Thirty minutes per sound","c":"每字三十分","ok":0,"note":"Far longer than the traditional practice and likely to cause light-headedness. 遠過古法，且易致眩。"},{"t":"Until you run out of air completely","c":"呼至氣盡","ok":0,"note":"Never exhale to the very end — that is the main thing to avoid in any breathing practice. 呼不可盡，此吐納諸法之首戒。"},{"t":"Once each, quickly","c":"每字一過而速","ok":0,"note":"Too brief to settle anything. 太促，不足以致其和。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"Where does the Six Healing Sounds practice come from?","qZh":"六字訣出於何處？","opts":[{"t":"It is recorded in the Southern dynasties by Tao Hongjing, and is among the oldest breathing methods still taught","c":"南朝陶弘景《養性延命錄》已載，為現存最古之吐納法之一","ok":1,"note":"Tao Hongjing 陶弘景 recorded it in the Yangxing Yanming Lu 養性延命錄 around the sixth century, and it has been transmitted continuously since, with the sound-to-organ pairings largely stable. Modern standardised versions exist alongside older lineages. 陶弘景《養性延命錄》約六世紀載之，其後傳承不絕，字臟之配大體無改。今有標準化之編，與古傳並行。"},{"t":"It was invented in the twentieth century","c":"二十世紀所創","ok":0,"note":"Modern standardised routines exist, but the practice itself is very old. 今雖有標準化之套，然其法甚古。"},{"t":"It comes from Indian pranayama","c":"出於印度調息之法","ok":0,"note":"Different tradition, though breathing practices exist in many cultures. 別為一系。然調息之法，諸文明皆有。"},{"t":"It has no textual source","c":"無典可稽","ok":0,"note":"It has a clear and early textual source. 典有明文，且出甚早。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"What should you do if you feel light-headed during breathing practice?","qZh":"行吐納而覺頭暈，當如何？","opts":[{"t":"Stop, breathe normally, and sit down until it passes","c":"即止，復其常息，坐而待其安","ok":1,"note":"Light-headedness usually means the breath has been made too deep, too long, or held. Return to ordinary breathing immediately; sit if you need to. If it happens repeatedly, or with chest pain or palpitations, that is a reason to speak with a clinician rather than to practise harder. 眩者，多因息過深、過長或有所閉。當即復其常息，必要則坐。若屢作，或兼胸痛心悸，宜就醫而不宜益練。"},{"t":"Breathe faster to compensate","c":"急息以應之","ok":0,"note":"That makes it worse. 反甚。"},{"t":"Hold the breath longer","c":"更久閉氣","ok":0,"note":"Never. 斷不可。"},{"t":"Continue and it will pass","c":"續行自止","ok":0,"note":"Do not push through this particular symptom. 此症不可強行。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"Why is XĪ 嘻 (San Jiao) placed last?","qZh":"「嘻」何以居末？","opts":[{"t":"The San Jiao runs through all three burners, so it harmonises what the previous five have moved","c":"三焦貫上中下，故以之調和前五字之所動","ok":1,"note":"The set follows the same logic as Eight Brocades: five specific sounds, then one that gathers. The San Jiao is the passage for qi and fluids through the whole trunk, so it is the natural closing sound. 其理與八段錦同：先五者各有所專，末一者總收之。三焦為一身氣水之道，故以之為收。"},{"t":"Because it is the least important","c":"以其最輕","ok":0,"note":"Closing is not a lesser role — it is what keeps the practice from leaving you unsettled. 收非小事，正所以免其散而不安。"},{"t":"Because the San Jiao is not an organ","c":"以三焦非臟","ok":0,"note":"The San Jiao is a fu organ with a channel of its own. 三焦為腑，且自有其經。"},{"t":"Because it is hardest to pronounce","c":"以其音最難","ok":0,"note":"Placement follows function, not difficulty. 其位從功用而定，不從難易。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the relationship between 吐納 tu na and 導引 daoyin?","qZh":"吐納與導引，其別安在？","opts":[{"t":"Tu na is breath work; daoyin adds guided movement and attention to it","c":"吐納主息，導引則兼形與意","ok":1,"note":"They overlap constantly in practice — most daoyin sets contain breathing instruction, and most breathing practices include some posture. The distinction is one of emphasis: tu na works primarily on the breath, daoyin on breath plus movement plus intention. 二者常相涵：導引之套多有息法，吐納之行亦有其形。其別在偏重：吐納主息，導引則形、息、意三者並行。"},{"t":"They are unrelated traditions","c":"二者不相涉","ok":0,"note":"They come from the same body of practice and share texts. 同出一脈，且共其典。"},{"t":"Tu na is only done lying down","c":"吐納惟臥而行","ok":0,"note":"It is done sitting, standing or lying. 坐、立、臥皆可。"},{"t":"Daoyin does not involve breathing","c":"導引不涉呼吸","ok":0,"note":"Breath is one of its three components. 息為其三要之一。"}]}]};
